WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.767
-Former President Donald Trump

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:04.334
ended an interview with NPR

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:07.400
by abruptly hanging up
after he was fact-checked

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.234
on his lies
about the 2020 election.

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:11.534
For more on this,
it's time for "A Closer Look."

00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:15.367
♪♪

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:19.100
Well, we got two more days
in our undisclosed location

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.601
before heading back
to the studio

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:21.934
and I want to say
how nice it was

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:24.534
that so many of you
asked me if I was okay.

00:00:24.534 --> 00:00:25.734
It was less nice,

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:29.400
how many of you asked me
if Wally was okay,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.367
since Wally
didn't test positive,

00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:33.234
but since you're so concerned,

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:35.434
here's a shot of him right now
in the studio.

00:00:36.801 --> 00:00:40.567
So there you have it, everyone,
your precious Wally is okay.

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.667
You can all breathe easy,

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.601
something I can't do because,
unlike Wally,

00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:47.200
I had [bleep] COVID.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.133
I'm joking, of course.

00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:49.601
I had no symptoms,

00:00:49.601 --> 00:00:51.467
thanks to my two vaccines
and a booster

00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:53.267
and no thanks to this --

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:56.400
this horse pudding that
I accidentally bought

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.801
when my corner bodega/
livestock supply store

00:00:59.801 --> 00:01:01.067
ran out of horse paste.

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:03.701
It is not good.

00:01:03.701 --> 00:01:05.367
Has no medicinal value.

00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:06.934
Plus, it's giving me
high blood pressure

00:01:06.934 --> 00:01:08.334
because it's so packed
with sugar

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.701
to cover up the taste of horse.

00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:14.400
What's that?
Today's flavor?

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.601
Seabiscuits and gravy.

00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:20.634
And, while I'm alone
in this apartment,

00:01:20.634 --> 00:01:22.567
I want to, once again,
thank artist Mary LaGarde

00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:24.300
for my Sea Captain
and Rihanna painting,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.100
which has been
keeping me company.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.133
I'm sorry I forgot to mention
you guys yesterday.

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:29.601
-Well, it didn't bother me,

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:31.834
but Rihanna did think
you were being a...

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:33.100
-♪ Rude boy, boy ♪

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.434
-Well, won't happen again.

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:36.801
Now, to the news at hand.

00:01:36.801 --> 00:01:39.100
-Hey, uh, Seth.
-Mm-hmm?

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.000
-Sorry to be a pail,
but just want to ask again.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.234
I know I record my line
every day for "A Closer Look,"

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:46.968
but I still can't
shake the feeling

00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:48.868
that you're just
taking her lines

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:51.567
from previously recorded songs.

00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:52.434
-Why do you say that?

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:54.167
-Well, you know, there's like

00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:55.767
music underneath it.

00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:56.968
-Oh, that's just a coincidence.

00:01:56.968 --> 00:01:58.501
-Okay.

00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:02.367
-The real "Closer Look"
is going to start

00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:03.934
now.

00:02:03.934 --> 00:02:05.133
Donald Trump has, of course,

00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:06.968
done many phone interviews
before,

00:02:06.968 --> 00:02:08.934
during and after his presidency,

00:02:08.934 --> 00:02:10.734
many with the Fox &amp; Friends,

00:02:10.734 --> 00:02:13.100
who tend to sit there
with blank looks on their faces,

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.901
like they're
Boston Dynamics robots

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:16.567
who haven't been
switched on yet.

00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:19.067
There was even that one time
he said, out of the blue,

00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:20.667
that he was going to come on
once a week

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:23.868
and even they did not seem
super psyched about that idea.

00:02:23.868 --> 00:02:27.267
-President Trump, welcome back
to "Fox &amp; Friends."

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:28.501
-Well, thank you very much.

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:29.767
Good to be here.

00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:31.133
Great to be with my friends.

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:32.801
I think we're going to do this,
we've agreed to do it

00:02:32.801 --> 00:02:35.000
once a week in the morning
and I look forward to it.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.300
We're going to do it every week,
every Monday, I think they said,

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.400
and, if we can't do it
on a Monday,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.734
we'll do it on a Tuesday,
like we did today.

00:02:41.734 --> 00:02:43.267
-Alright.
-Sounds good.

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:45.300
-Okay.
-Mr. President,
thank you very much.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.167
You may want to do it
every week,

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:48.934
but Fox has not committed
to that.

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:51.300
We're going to take it
on a case-by-case basis.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.601
-I love how Trump offered Monday
as an option

00:02:53.601 --> 00:02:57.167
and Tuesday as a backup,
if Monday didn't work for them.

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:58.300
The president
should never be the one

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.067
who's flexible on schedule.

00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:02.133
[ As Trump ]
Look, you guys are the ones
doing a TV show every day.

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:03.934
I'm just watching one.

00:03:03.934 --> 00:03:06.033
But, for some reason,
he decided to forgo

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:09.634
both his Fox and his Friends
to call into NPR.

00:03:09.634 --> 00:03:10.968
NPR?!

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:12.133
How is that even possible?

00:03:12.133 --> 00:03:14.734
Are their microphones
even calibrated

00:03:14.734 --> 00:03:17.734
to capture sounds
at his decibel level?

00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:19.000
Their most popular show is...

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:23.400
I mean, a good way to describe
the timbre and pitch

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.801
of Donald Trump's voice is
reverse Terry Gross.

00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:29.000
[ As Gross ]
We're joined now by the author

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.300
of "The Art of the Deal,"
Donald Trump.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.601
Welcome to the show, Donald.

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:32.901
[ As Trump ]
Thanks for being --

00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:33.834
Great to be here.

00:03:33.834 --> 00:03:37.801
Ee oo ee oo ee oo oo ee!

00:03:37.801 --> 00:03:41.200
Also, I'm pretty sure
it's a rule

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.734
that you have to whisper on NPR.

00:03:42.734 --> 00:03:44.501
Otherwise, you'll wake up
the kindly old man

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:45.934
whose house they broadcast
out of.

00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:47.601
His name is Bert Wilson.

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:49.267
He lives in Minnewaska,
North Dakota,

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:51.100
where he recently retired
after 50 years

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.334
as a teller
at the old community bank

00:03:53.334 --> 00:03:55.734
and, now, he sits upstairs
in his rocking chair,

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:57.968
watching "The Guns of Navarone"
on LaserDisc,

00:03:57.968 --> 00:03:59.467
while the kids from NPR

00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:01.334
record their little show
out of his living room.

00:04:01.334 --> 00:04:04.434
Anyway, Trump calling in to NPR
is like Ric Flair guest hosting

00:04:04.434 --> 00:04:06.267
an episode of BBC's...

00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:08.434
[ As Flair ]
We're going to see some snakes.

00:04:08.434 --> 00:04:12.100
We're going to see some parrots
and you can bet your ass

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.100
we're going to see

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.868
some mother[bleep]
flying squirrels.

00:04:15.868 --> 00:04:18.067
Slick Ric, whoo!

00:04:18.067 --> 00:04:20.434
The point is, for some reason,

00:04:20.434 --> 00:04:22.701
Trump thought it would be
a good idea to call in to NPR,

00:04:22.701 --> 00:04:25.400
where he seemed to be
caught off-guard

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.133
when he was grilled about his
2020 election fraud lies

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:30.234
by host Steve Inskeep.

00:04:30.234 --> 00:04:33.400
It's clear Trump forgot
the first rule of NPR.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.701
Steve plays for Inskeeps.

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:39.934
This is nice.

00:04:39.934 --> 00:04:43.100
Like I miss the studio,
but it's nice to --

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:47.367
be here with my horse pudding
and tell a joke like that.

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:49.701
Because let's be honest --
if I'd

00:04:49.701 --> 00:04:52.734
done the play for Inskeeps joke
in front of an audience,

00:04:52.734 --> 00:04:53.934
they'd be looking
at me like this --

00:04:55.834 --> 00:04:58.033
Point is, instead of listening,
mouth agape,

00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:00.267
to Trump's answers
while giving no pushback,

00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:03.567
Inskeep came prepared with basic
facts to debunk Trump's claims

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:06.234
and Trump clearly had no idea
how to handle it.

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:07.834
To start with,
he didn't even seem to know

00:05:07.834 --> 00:05:10.200
the details of how the interview
was going to work.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.200
According to the transcript,
Trump asked Inskeep,

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.367
at the start of the interview...

00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:24.133
Trump should write a book
called...

00:05:24.133 --> 00:05:27.334
...with tips like, "Always find
out the interview specifications

00:05:27.334 --> 00:05:29.534
after it's too late
to change them."

00:05:29.534 --> 00:05:32.534
He's a former president calling
in to a well-known news program

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:34.300
and he acts like he just got
stopped on the street

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.133
by Jay Leno.

00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:36.234
[ As Trump ]
Whoa!

00:05:36.234 --> 00:05:38.601
Oh, I'm on TV right now?

00:05:38.601 --> 00:05:40.534
So, it was not
an auspicious start

00:05:40.534 --> 00:05:41.734
to the interview for Trump

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:43.234
and it quickly went downhill
from there

00:05:43.234 --> 00:05:45.667
when Inskeep presented him
with some very basic facts

00:05:45.667 --> 00:05:47.701
that contradict his lies
about the 2020 election

00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:49.901
and Trump clearly did not know
how to respond.

00:05:49.901 --> 00:05:52.567
For example,
when Trump brought up Arizona,

00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:55.167
where even a GOP-backed
sham audit

00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:58.234
conducted by his own allies
confirmed Biden's win,

00:05:58.234 --> 00:06:01.534
Inskeep fact-checked him
and Trump was really flustered.

00:06:01.534 --> 00:06:02.667
-If you look at the numbers,

00:06:02.667 --> 00:06:05.334
if you look
at the findings in Arizona,

00:06:05.334 --> 00:06:08.400
if you look at what's going on
in Georgia, Pennsylvania,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.567
and Wisconsin, by the way,
and take a look at Wisconsin,

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:13.868
they're finding things
that nobody thought possible.

00:06:13.868 --> 00:06:15.601
This was a corrupt election.

00:06:15.601 --> 00:06:17.901
-Doug Logan,
who ran the audit in Arizona

00:06:17.901 --> 00:06:21.267
that was set up by your allies,
didn't find serious problems.

00:06:21.267 --> 00:06:23.534
This is a quote -- "The ballots
that were provided to us

00:06:23.534 --> 00:06:26.234
to count in the Coliseum
very accurately correlate

00:06:26.234 --> 00:06:28.601
with the official
canvass numbers."

00:06:28.601 --> 00:06:30.234
He raised a bunch
of administrative issues,

00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:31.934
but didn't find a problem

00:06:31.934 --> 00:06:33.601
that would've changed
the result at all.

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:35.534
-The ballots may correspond,

00:06:35.534 --> 00:06:37.801
but look at the ballots
themselves.

00:06:37.801 --> 00:06:40.033
The number of ballots
doesn't mean anything.

00:06:40.033 --> 00:06:41.067
-[ As Trump ]
Look at the numbers.

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:42.300
"I'm looking at the numbers

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.300
and they're the opposite
of what you say they are."

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.868
[ As Trump ]
Then you got to stop looking
at the numbers.

00:06:46.868 --> 00:06:48.801
They're going
to drive you crazy.

00:06:48.801 --> 00:06:50.734
They're just numbers
and a number has never --

00:06:50.734 --> 00:06:52.968
Steve, a number's never going
to love you back.

00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:54.734
You got to get outside.
You got to look at the clouds,

00:06:54.734 --> 00:06:56.100
look at the sky.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.334
You got to live.

00:06:57.334 --> 00:06:58.801
Don't look at the numbers.

00:06:58.801 --> 00:06:59.868
Also, I love this shift

00:06:59.868 --> 00:07:01.934
to "Look at the ballots
themselves."

00:07:01.934 --> 00:07:03.767
That's what they did.

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:07.434
That was the whole point
of this bogus audit

00:07:07.434 --> 00:07:10.033
with their dumb UV lights
and all their insane theories

00:07:10.033 --> 00:07:12.334
about secret watermarks
and bamboo fibers.

00:07:12.334 --> 00:07:13.567
They looked at the ballots.

00:07:13.567 --> 00:07:14.634
What do you think
they were doing,

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:16.400
looking at the stamps
on the envelopes?

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.634
"Flag, flag, moon landing, flag.

00:07:20.634 --> 00:07:22.501
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa!

00:07:22.501 --> 00:07:24.200
Jimi Hendrix.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.033
Shred that one."

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:28.567
And then, when Inskeep
brought up the fact that,

00:07:28.567 --> 00:07:31.000
even the people who backed
and conducted the audit

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.667
accepted the conclusion
that Biden won,

00:07:32.667 --> 00:07:33.801
Trump dismissed them

00:07:33.801 --> 00:07:36.267
as Republicans in name only,
or RINOs.

00:07:36.267 --> 00:07:38.434
-Why did Republican officials
in Arizona

00:07:38.434 --> 00:07:40.133
accept the results, then?

00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:42.067
-Because they're RINOs
and, frankly,

00:07:42.067 --> 00:07:43.467
a lot of people
are questioning that.

00:07:43.467 --> 00:07:45.000
-Apparently, a RINO is now

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.334
anyone who doesn't show
full obedience to Trump.

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:49.000
It's a label so scary
to Republicans that,

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.767
in order to avoid it, Ted Cruz,
who helped lead the effort

00:07:51.767 --> 00:07:54.267
to overthrow the results
on January 6th,

00:07:54.267 --> 00:07:56.467
had to grovel
and beg for forgiveness

00:07:56.467 --> 00:07:58.133
for the simple act
of calling the insurrection

00:07:58.133 --> 00:07:59.300
a violent terrorist attack.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.200
In order to prove
he wasn't a RINO

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.467
and that he was extreme enough
for Trump and his base,

00:08:03.467 --> 00:08:05.868
Cruz had to perform
an act of self-humiliation

00:08:05.868 --> 00:08:07.334
live on national television

00:08:07.334 --> 00:08:10.167
even Steve-O wouldn't have
subjected himself to.

00:08:10.167 --> 00:08:12.901
But, Steve-O,
you stapled your ass.

00:08:12.901 --> 00:08:15.801
[ As Steve-O ]
And I'd steal it shut
with Super Glue

00:08:15.801 --> 00:08:18.000
before stooping to that level.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:18.834
Well, you know what?

00:08:18.834 --> 00:08:20.634
We respect you, Steve-O.

00:08:20.634 --> 00:08:23.200
Now, let's get you into that
exploding Porta Potti.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.434
Anyway, as we've pointed out
many times on this show,

00:08:25.434 --> 00:08:27.701
even pro-Trump judges
and lawyers have admitted

00:08:27.701 --> 00:08:29.467
that there was no evidence
for his claims,

00:08:29.467 --> 00:08:32.133
which is why all of his court
challenges failed.

00:08:32.133 --> 00:08:33.901
And, when Inskeep
brought that up directly

00:08:33.901 --> 00:08:36.100
to the former president,
he, again, had no response.

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:38.200
-Let me read you
some short quotes.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:40.267
The first is by one
of the judges,

00:08:40.267 --> 00:08:42.501
one of the ten judges
you appointed who ruled on this.

00:08:42.501 --> 00:08:45.334
And there were many judges,
but ten who you appointed.

00:08:45.334 --> 00:08:47.367
Brett Ludwig U.S. District Court
in Wisconsin,

00:08:47.367 --> 00:08:49.501
who was nominated by you
in 2020.

00:08:49.501 --> 00:08:50.834
He's on the bench and he says,

00:08:50.834 --> 00:08:53.534
"This court allowed
the plaintiff the chance

00:08:53.534 --> 00:08:55.901
to make his case
and he has lost on the merits."

00:08:55.901 --> 00:08:57.367
Another quote, Kory Langhofer,

00:08:57.367 --> 00:09:00.834
for your own campaign attorney
in Arizona, November 12, 2020,

00:09:00.834 --> 00:09:03.601
"We are not alleging fraud
in this lawsuit.

00:09:03.601 --> 00:09:06.100
We are not alleging anyone
stealing the election."

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.234
And also Rudy Giuliani,
your lawyer,

00:09:08.234 --> 00:09:10.133
November 18, 2020
in Pennsylvania,

00:09:10.133 --> 00:09:12.434
"This is not a fraud case."

00:09:12.434 --> 00:09:14.667
Your own lawyers
had no evidence of fraud.

00:09:14.667 --> 00:09:17.167
They said in court
they had no evidence of fraud

00:09:17.167 --> 00:09:20.234
and the judges ruled against you
every time on the merits.

00:09:20.234 --> 00:09:22.067
-It was too early
to ask for fraud

00:09:22.067 --> 00:09:23.634
and to talk about fraud.

00:09:23.634 --> 00:09:26.400
Rudy said that because of the
fact it was very early

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.701
with the --
because that was obviously

00:09:28.701 --> 00:09:30.968
at a very, very --
that was a long time ago.

00:09:30.968 --> 00:09:33.000
-What do you mean,
it was too early for fraud?

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.567
Like the fraud groundhog emerged
from his hole

00:09:35.567 --> 00:09:36.767
and saw his shadow,

00:09:36.767 --> 00:09:39.000
meaning you had to wait
six weeks for fraud?

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.067
Also, is this
the fraud groundhog?

00:09:41.067 --> 00:09:44.133
Because that would make
a good amount of sense.

00:09:44.133 --> 00:09:45.400
And, if it was too early
for fraud,

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:47.534
then why were your lawyers even
in court at all?

00:09:47.534 --> 00:09:48.534
What was the point of all that?

00:09:48.534 --> 00:09:50.234
Was Rudy Giuliani just
getting in

00:09:50.234 --> 00:09:53.367
a few last lawsuits
before his neck implant surgery?

00:09:53.367 --> 00:09:54.367
[ As Giuliani ]
Good news, boss.

00:09:54.367 --> 00:09:55.367
They found a donor for me.

00:09:55.367 --> 00:09:57.367
He lives at The Bronx Zoo.

00:09:57.367 --> 00:09:59.534
You wouldn't like it there.

00:09:59.534 --> 00:10:01.567
They got rhinos.
[ Laughs ]

00:10:01.567 --> 00:10:03.067
And then, of course,

00:10:03.067 --> 00:10:05.234
the interview came
to its inevitable conclusion

00:10:05.234 --> 00:10:07.200
when Trump abruptly hung up

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:09.934
after one too many
uncomfortable fact-checks.

00:10:09.934 --> 00:10:12.133
-People have no idea how big
this issue is

00:10:12.133 --> 00:10:14.167
and they don't want it
to happen again.

00:10:14.167 --> 00:10:15.734
It shouldn't be allowed
to happen

00:10:15.734 --> 00:10:17.300
and they don't want it
to happen again.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:18.801
-I want to --
-And the only way it's not going

00:10:18.801 --> 00:10:22.701
to happen again is
you have to solve the problem

00:10:22.701 --> 00:10:25.534
of the presidential
rigged election of 2020.

00:10:25.534 --> 00:10:27.033
-Mr. President, if I --
-So, Steve, thank you very much.

00:10:27.033 --> 00:10:28.200
-Whoa, whoa, whoa!
I have one more question.
-I appreciate it.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.868
-I want to ask about a court
hearing yesterday

00:10:29.868 --> 00:10:31.200
on January 6th.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.601
Judge Amit Mehta --
He's gone. Okay.

00:10:33.601 --> 00:10:36.334
-Wow. That's the most impolite
thing to happen on NPR

00:10:36.334 --> 00:10:37.567
since Ira Glass farted

00:10:37.567 --> 00:10:39.300
during an episode
of "This American Life."

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:42.467
In fairness,
they are running out of topics.

00:10:42.467 --> 00:10:45.400
[ As Glass ]
Farts -- they're part
of our lives,

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:48.000
albeit an unsavory part.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.567
It can be grim.

00:10:49.567 --> 00:10:51.300
Sometimes they're funny.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.467
Sometimes they linger.

00:10:53.467 --> 00:10:56.467
And farts -- our topic today.

00:10:56.467 --> 00:11:00.701
Ira Glass, Terry Gross,
Steve-O, and Ric Flair?

00:11:00.701 --> 00:11:02.868
It's called range.
Look it up.

00:11:02.868 --> 00:11:05.601
It's not easy fitting all those
references under one...

00:11:05.601 --> 00:11:07.133
-♪ Umbrella ♪

00:11:07.133 --> 00:11:10.300
-Oh, you know what?
I do think --

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:13.234
I do think those lines are
from songs.

00:11:13.234 --> 00:11:14.701
You know it's bad
when Donald Trump,

00:11:14.701 --> 00:11:17.267
who will drone on endlessly
for as long as you let him,

00:11:17.267 --> 00:11:20.400
willingly ends an interview
before it's supposed to be over.

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:22.033
When these deranged lies
are subjected

00:11:22.033 --> 00:11:23.801
to even a modicum of scrutiny,

00:11:23.801 --> 00:11:25.934
they collapse because they're
absurd on their face

00:11:25.934 --> 00:11:27.734
and, yet, an entire
political party,

00:11:27.734 --> 00:11:30.100
an ideological movement,
including the likes of Ted Cruz

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:31.434
and the right-wing media
apparatus,

00:11:31.434 --> 00:11:33.100
are all devoted
to repeating those lies

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:35.734
and anyone who balks at them
even slightly

00:11:35.734 --> 00:11:38.033
is attacked,
purged from the ranks.

00:11:38.033 --> 00:11:40.133
As for us, we'll be back
in the studio next week,

00:11:40.133 --> 00:11:41.567
starting on Monday and...

00:11:41.567 --> 00:11:43.934
-If we can do it on a Monday,
we'll do it on a Tuesday.

00:11:43.934 --> 00:11:45.868
-This has been "A Closer Look."

00:11:45.868 --> 00:11:49.434
♪♪

